A5: AGGLOMERATIVE HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING

AIM: Group similar documents without labels using bottom-up hierarchical clustering, and draw the dendrogram.

CODE EXPLAINED
1. matplotlib.use("Agg")
   Makes matplotlib save images instead of opening a window (works without a display). Delete this line if you want plt.show().

2. docs = [...]
   9 short documents on three topics: sports, AI/ML, finance. No labels given to the algorithm.

3. vec = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words="english"); X = vec.fit_transform(docs).toarray()
   Converts each document into a TF-IDF vector (weights important words higher). .toarray() makes it a dense matrix because Ward linkage needs dense data.

4. model = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=3, linkage="ward"); labels = model.fit_predict(X)
   Starts with 9 clusters (one per doc), repeatedly merges the two clusters whose merge increases variance the least (Ward), until 3 remain. labels[i] = cluster number of doc i.

5. Printing loop
   For each cluster number, prints the documents that have that label.

6. Z = linkage(X, method="ward")
   scipy computes the full merge history (which clusters merged, at what distance). This is the data for the tree.

7. dendrogram(Z, labels=[D1..D9]); plt.savefig("dendrogram.png")
   Draws the tree. Bottom = documents, height = distance at which clusters merged. Saved as dendrogram.png.

PIPELINE: text -> TF-IDF -> agglomerative clustering -> clusters + dendrogram.

OUTPUT TO EXPECT: 3 clusters printed, dendrogram.png created. Not perfect because the data is tiny (the neural networks doc landed with finance).

VIVA Q&A
Q: What is clustering? Clustering vs classification?
A: Grouping similar items without labels (unsupervised). Classification needs labeled training data.

Q: What is agglomerative clustering?
A: Bottom-up hierarchical method: each doc starts as its own cluster; the two closest clusters are merged repeatedly.

Q: Agglomerative vs divisive?
A: Agglomerative merges (bottom-up); divisive splits (top-down).

Q: What is a dendrogram?
A: A tree showing the order of merges and the distance of each merge. Cutting it at a height gives clusters.

Q: What is linkage? Types?
A: Rule for distance between clusters: single (nearest points), complete (farthest), average, ward (minimum variance increase).

Q: Why ward?
A: Gives compact, balanced clusters; common default.

Q: Why TF-IDF instead of counts?
A: Down-weights common words, so document similarity is more meaningful.

Q: How do you choose the number of clusters?
A: Cut the dendrogram at a big jump in merge distance, or use silhouette score. Here k=3 was fixed.

Q: Advantages and disadvantages?
A: No need to know k in advance, gives a hierarchy. But O(n^2) memory/time and merges can't be undone.

Q: Compare with K-means.
A: K-means needs k, is faster on large data. Hierarchical gives the whole tree and is deterministic.

Q: What distance is used?
A: Euclidean distance between TF-IDF vectors (Ward uses it). Cosine is common for text with other linkages.

Q: Why .toarray()?
A: TF-IDF returns a sparse matrix; Ward linkage needs a dense one.

